d orthogonal.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of fractional factorial designs, as referenced in Table 9.19, relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The effects of the factors are additive and linear.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent.\n4. The factors are fixed and not random.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFractional factorial designs are particularly useful when:\n- The number of factors is large, making a full factorial design impractical.\n- The researcher is interested in screening factors to identify significant ones.\n- Resources (time, budget, materials) are limited, necessitating a reduction in the number of runs.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data from fractional factorial designs typically involves:\n1. Estimating the effects of factors using ANOVA or regression analysis.\n2. Conducting hypothesis tests to determine the significance of main effects and interactions.\n3. Using graphical methods (e.g., interaction plots) to visualize the effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Significant reduction in the number of experimental runs compared to full factorial designs.\n- Ability to identify important factors and interactions efficiently.\n- Flexibility in design, allowing for the exploration of multiple factors simultaneously.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Potential for confounding effects, where the effects of some factors may be indistinguishable from others.\n- Limited ability to estimate higher-order interactions unless specifically designed to do so.\n- Requires careful selection of factors to avoid aliasing.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include full factorial designs, Plackett-Burman designs for screening, and response surface methodology for optimization. Alternatives may involve using central composite designs or Box-Behnken designs for more detailed